routes.brief:
Feng–Yun–Zhang, Invent. Math. 235 (2023), 569–668, prove a function-field analogue of the Kudla–Rapoport conjecture that computes *every* Taylor coefficient of a non-singular Fourier coefficient of a Siegel–Eisenstein series as the degree of a special cycle on the moduli of Hermitian shtukas. GlobalShtukasAndFunctionFieldLanglands builds Bun_G, Hecke and shtuka stacks and their cohomology for the Langlands correspondence (GS.0–GS.7); it plans no cycles on those stacks, so this is new work in its direction. Plan it together with the Yun–Zhang PGL_2 theory it generalizes, whose Octahedron Lemma and Hecke-action computation it imports.

Import, never re-plan:
- GS.0–GS.2 for Bun_{U(n)}, the Hecke stacks Hk^r_{U(n)} and the shtuka stacks Sht^r_{U(n)} (the Hermitian-bundle description is the U(n) case; Lemma 6.7, emptiness for odd r, is specific and belongs here);
- SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.5 for Chow groups of stacks, refined Gysin maps and intersection products (Kresch for Artin stacks);
- EtaleDualityAndPerverseSheaves EDC.5 and EDC.7 for the perverse t-structure, intermediate extensions, smallness and the decomposition theorem; EDC.8 and WeightsInEtaleCohomology R34.1 for cohomological correspondences, the Grothendieck–Lefschetz trace formula and the sheaf-to-function dictionary;
- GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.3 for the Hermitian local densities and the Cho–Yamauchi formula, and FunctionFieldArithmetic FA.6 for the Siegel–Eisenstein series and its Fourier expansion (both routed as sources by this same extraction).

Construct:
- Springer theory over curves: Laumon's Springer sheaf on Coh_d(X) with its S_d-action and Springer fibres (§3), and its Hermitian analogue on Herm_{2d}(X′/X) with the W_d = (Z/2)^d ⋊ S_d action, the local model [o_{2d}/O_{2d}], the Lagrangian correspondence and the stalk comparison with Coh_d(X) (§4);
- the density sheaf K^{Eis}_d(T) and the geometrization Den(T, Q) = Tr(Fr, K^{Eis}_d(T)_Q) (§5);
- the special cycles Z^r_E(a) on Sht^r_{U(n)}, their finiteness and properness, the expected-dimension theorem for corank one, good framings and the cycle class [Z^r_E(a)] (§§7, 9);
- the Hitchin-type stacks M(m, n), the Hitchin fibration, its smallness through υ_{2d} : Lagr_{2d} → Herm_{2d}, Hitchin shtukas and the intrinsic class [Sht^r_{M_d}] (§8);
- the comparison of the two cycle classes (Theorem 10.1), through auxiliary Hitchin stacks, étale comparisons and the Octahedron Lemma (§10).

The final theorems: the trace formula for Hitchin shtukas (Proposition 11.8), the spectral decomposition K^{Int}_d(T) = ⊕_iSpr^{Herm}_{2d}[ρ_i]T^i with the Hecke correspondence acting by (d−2i) (Proposition 11.7), the geometric functional equation (Lemmas 11.11–11.13), the matching K^{Int}_d ≅ K^{Eis}_d (Proposition 12.3) through the W_d-identity of Lemma 12.4, and Theorem 1.1 with its split-case form (§12.3).

Keep the hypotheses visible: char k ≠ 2, ν : X′ → X étale (the ramified case appears only in §4.1 and Remark 4.8), the Hermitian pairing valued in ω_{X′}, and a injective. Remark 1.2 records what happens for r = 0 (classical Siegel–Weil), r = 1 (empty under these unramifiedness assumptions, matching the +1 sign) and the sign −1 variant of the companion paper.

Tests:
- r = 0 against the classical Siegel–Weil formula;
- odd r: both sides vanish (Lemma 6.7 and Corollary 11.14);
- Lemma 12.4 and the decomposition (11.5) with its irreducibility, verified here by character computation for d ≤ 4;
- the split case X′ = X ⊔ X against the GL_n shtuka picture of Yun–Zhang (§12.3).

Review additions: the U(n)-specific Lemmas 6.7 and 6.9 (item 34), with the corrected parity argument for X′ = X_{k′}; the modification convention of Definitions 6.3 and 6.6 (lower at x′_i, upper at σ(x′_i)); the ω_{X′}-dual convention with F_1 = ν^*ω_X^{1/2}; Lemma 10.5 only for w_1 and h_1, with the corrected argument through M_{d,e} ⊂ M_d ×_{X_d} (X_{d−e} × X_e)^{disj} (E22); Lemma 10.10 on the open part Z^♡; and Lemma 12.4 with sgn̄_j.
